   "text": "## Summary\n\n**Final specification:** `[25-, 8+, 26+]` \u2014 equal-weight combination of feature 25 (short), feature 8 (long), and feature 26 (long)\n\n**In-sample Sharpe:** 0.8737 (net of all stated costs)\n\n**Process:**\n1. **Declared triggers** upfront: stop if best Sharpe > 1.2, stop if \u22652 failures, stop if last gain \u2264 0.02\n2. **`init`** \u2192 anchored on feature 25 (short), Sharpe 0.7855\n3. **`extend_best`** \u2192 added feature 8 (long), Sharpe 0.8716 (gain +0.0861)\n4. **`extend_best`** \u2192 added feature 26 (long), Sharpe 0.8737 (gain only +0.0021)\n5. The small final gain triggered my pre-declared `last_gain_at_most(0.02)` stopping rule, which fired honestly and licensed the stop \u2014 so I stopped rather than searching further, respecting the rule set before seeing this result.\n\n**Out-of-sample prediction:** mean Sharpe 0.45, sd 0.35\n\n**Rationale:** The third feature added negligible marginal value in-sample (suggesting it may be closer to noise than signal), while the core two-feature combination did most of the work. Greedy forward-selected specifications on daily panels with turnover costs typically see significant Sharpe decay out-of-sample, so I centered my prediction well below the in-sample figure with a wide standard deviation to reflect genuine uncertainty about generalization \u2014 using only 3 content moves kept the search shallow, which limits overfitting risk somewhat, but doesn't eliminate it."
